(This is crossposted to comp.unix.osf.osf1 and comp.sys.dec; I have
 directed followups to comp.sys.dec; apologies if there's a more
 appropriate newsgroup for this).

I have an Alpha 3000/300 running OSF/1 (1.2) connected to a moderate
size network of Suns, IBM RS/6000's, and one DecStation 3100.  In the
course of porting a Fortran application to the Alpha, it became clear
that there were unacceptable delays associated with the INQUIRE
function.  

Further study showed that the RPC "readdir" operation was generated by
this INQUIRE, that the problem could be duplicated via a simple "ls"
command from Unix in the directory that had the relevant file, and that
it only occured if the character count of the "ls" listing was in excess
of 8192 bytes (or so).  The nfsstat -rc command showed bad calls and
timeouts (usually two of each) occur when the "ls" command was given.
This occured regardless of whether the NFS server was a Sun (SunOS 4 or
5), IBM RS/6k (AIX 3.2) or DecStation (Ultrix 4.3).

I called Dec S/W support, talked to a very helpful person there who
immediately suspected hardware (the RPC errors were all incoming, none
outgoing) and so I called the H/W support.  To cut a long story short,
we identified the external transceiver that converts twisted pair to
thinwire as the culprit.  The model we were using was a Milan MIL120A,
and the symptoms completely vanished when an Allied Telesys CentreCom
model AT-MR12X was used (or is it MR122T?  This is printed on the front,
but the part # on the underneath has the former designation).

Has anyone else experienced problems like this?  I should emphasize that
with the Milan transceiver in place, everything else seemed to work
fine, except those large directory lookups.  I really thought the
problem was in the nfsiod daemons on the Alpha but the transceiver swap
proved me wrong.  I guess somewhere there's an approved list of
transceivers from DEC but I haven't come across it yet.  Also, does
anyone have a clue as to _why_ the brand of transceiver should make a
difference?
				- Pat
