Well, after installing the 3.3.2 update DEC sent us, things did seem
OK, but soon afterward I found that the "-convert big_endian" problem
is *almost* fixed, but not quite totally.  Specifically, it seems to
work OK for integers (both scalars and arrays), for real scalars, and
for real arrays with explicit subscript limits.  But it fails on real
arrays with implicit subscript limits.  Unlike the previous problems
(which affected output but not input, I believe), this one hits both
input and output.  Take a look at the following four short programs;
the first three work, the fourth doesn't.  A workaround for whatever
programs break because of this bug is obvious, but it could be a bit
a nuisance to implement it in a really gigantic program ...

moose> cat ww1.f
	program w1
	real x1,x2
	data x1,x2/1.0,2.0/
	open(unit=1,file='spam',status='new',form='unformatted')
	write (1) x1,x2
	close(unit=1)
	call exit
	end
moose> 
moose> cat ww2.f
	program w2
	real x(2)
	data x/1.0,2.0/
	open(unit=1,file='spam',status='new',form='unformatted')
	write (1) x(1),x(2)
	close(unit=1)
	call exit
	end
moose> 
moose> cat ww3.f
	program w3
	real x(2)
	data x/1.0,2.0/
	open(unit=1,file='spam',status='new',form='unformatted')
	write (1) (x(i),i=1,2)
	close(unit=1)
	call exit
	end
moose> 
moose> cat ww4.f
	program w4
	real x(2)
	data x/1.0,2.0/
	open(unit=1,file='spam',status='new',form='unformatted')
	write (1) x
	close(unit=1)
	call exit
	end
moose> 
moose> for x in 1 2 3 4; do
moose>> f77 -convert big_endian -o ww$x ww$x.f
moose>> rm -f spam
moose>> ww$x
moose>> od -x spam
moose>> done
