I have heard a rumour that DEC are not going to port TPU to OSF/1.

Please tell me this is untrue. I cannot believe that DEC would do something
that is so guaranteed to anger its users.

One of the biggest reasons why some of our user base choose DEC 
workstations instead of Sun is that they prefer EVE to other editors.
Actually, most of them just use it in EDT mode.
I don't mean the previous statement to kick off another religious war about
which editor is the best one. Users ask for EVE and up until now, DEC have 
provided it. If they do not provide it on OSF/1, then I predict that 75% of
them will defect to Sun. I know you can buy third party EVE look-alikes, but
I have never found them to be quite as good as the real thing. Anyway, they
run on Sun workstations so why not do just that?

There is also the question of LSE. I use the LSE editor all the time when
programming. Many of our programmers do the same as we find its language
sensitive features improve our productivity. I presume that if TPU is not
ported, then LSE will not be ported either. Can LSE be made to work based
on a third party version of TPU? I don't know. 

Personally, I generally prefer DEC tools to those that I have tried from other
manufacturers. If this rumour is confirmed, then I will definitely be changing
my mind and possibly my workstation. If it proves to be unfounded, I will be
more than pleased.


Peter Allan
STARLINK Project
Rutherford Appleton Laboratory


