	I'm trying to simulate live video on a Alpha AXP 3000/400 with 64 meg
	of RAM and running OSF 1.2 (soon to be OSF 1.3) and X11R5.  I am
	running with my DISPLAY set to local:0, since I read  in the
	OSF news group that this causes the client/server communications
	to be performed using shared memory.  I am trying to display the
	video in a 512x512 motif drawing area widget.

	To achieve "live video"  I would ideally need to display about 30
	frames/sec(fps). I Would be happy with 15 to 20 fps.	Currently
	I am only getting about 5 fps so any improvement would be welcome.

	One of the problems I am having is that the data I receive from
	the camera contains 256 gray levels and my colormap only has 128
	gray levels (I can't use all 256 colormap entries because I
	need to maintain other colors on the display).  As a result, I
	need to map each frame into my colormap.  I am currently doing
	this using a lookup table.

	The other source of slowness is that the data I am getting is
	in ZPixmap format and the only way I know of to get that into
	a window is by using XCreateImage/XPutImage. I'm not sure how
	efficient the X Image routines are.  The O'Reilly Xlib Volume 1
	suggests that these routines may be a bit slow because they pay
	attention to bit/byte ordering. 

	The routine which calls the loadImage routine below is invoked
	as an Xt Timer Callback Proc, every update interval. Currently
	the smallest update interval I can maintain is 200 ms.

	There is one other limitation.  I cannot use a "hardware solution"
	such as a graphics card.  The goal of the software  is  to support
	an open system.

	Below is the chunk of code in question.  If anyone has any ideas
	for optimizations I would appreciate a response.  Responses
	can either be posted to this group or preferably mailed to
	me at lazlo@netcom.com.

	Thanks
	Bob Palermo (lazlo@netcom.com)
	
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	/*
	 *  zpix is a pointer to the data returned by the camera.  It
	 *  is a 512x512 buffer containing pixel values ranging from
	 *  0-255.
	 */
	loadImage(zpix)
	register unsigned char	*zpix;
	{
	    register int	   x;
	    XImage		   *ximage   = NULL;
	    register int	   imageSize = 512*512;

	    /*
	     * tmpZpix is a pointer to a previously allocated 512x512
	     * chunk of memory.
	     */
            register unsigned char *tmpZpix  = 512x512 chunk of memory

	    /*
	     * gsLut is a pointer to a 256 element array which maps
	     * 0-255 gray levels into 0-127 gray levels.  For example
	     * gsLut[0] and gsLut[1] correspond to the pixel value
	     * for gray level 0 and gsLut[254] and gsLut[255] correspond
	     * to the pixel value for gray level 127
	     */
	    register unsigned long *gsLut    = colormap lookup table



	    /*
             * map the image from the 0-255 to 0-127
	     */
	    for (x = 0; x < imageSize; x++) {
		tmpZpix[x] = gsLut[zpix[x]];
	    }	

		
	    ximage = XCreateImage(_dpy,_visual,_depth,ZPixmap,0,
				  (char *)tmpZpix, _width, _height, 8, 0);

	    XPutImage(_dpy, _daWin, _gc, ximage, 0, 0, 0, 0, _width, _height);
	}
	



