|> "He's dead, Jim. I'll get his tricorder. You take his wallet."


	Structures, pointers, longs (sizeof(x) == 8)
		on a mod 8 boundry
	int (sizeof(int) == 4)
		on a mod 4 boundry
	short (sizeof(short) == 2)
		on a mod 2 boundry
	char (sizeof(cahr) == 1)
		on any boundry

There is a pattern there somewhere :-)

Malloc always returns % 8

Almost all unaligned access errors I have dealt with on the Alpha
platform have been because the wrong thing was passed, for example:

func (long * l)
{
	*l = some_value;
}


main()
{
	int i;
	func(&i);
}

This code can generate an unaligned access, and can produce bad side
affects. For example, the compliler will write 64 bits into the address
that is passed to the function, but you only passed 32 bits worth of 
space, so something is getting squashed.

/Dave


