In article <CDEEto.LIz@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca>, jsbazine@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca (Steve Bazinet) writes:
|> The kind of information I am looking for is I am interested in the main features
|> of OSF/1.  What makes it standout from other UNIX OS's?
|> Also how does it compare to AIX and HP-UX, just a very general overview.

These days, the feature sets of the Unix-derived OS's are pretty darn
similar: POSIX-like programming interface, TCP/IP, NFS, X11, etc.
Sure, vendors offer their own 'value-add' to the basic core, but you
shouldn't get too reliant on any one vendor's value add:  once you do,
they have you locked in.

The only thing I find that varies enough to matter is the system
administration interfaces.  I haven't used HP so I can't comment.  Most
of the DEC OSF is fairly close to the BSD style admin stuff; OSF/1 is
derived from Mach which uses BSD code for its Unix personality.  In
fact, some people prefer OSF/1 to Solaris 2 just because they like the
BSD sysadm style and they hate the SVR4 sysadm style used by Solaris
2.

AIX really went off onto their own tangent; they invented a database to
hold most every parameter related to system configuration and they
created their own set of commands to do system admin and one master
user interface that calls the set of commands.  It's different from
other Unix environments; some people love it and other people hate it.

