 We have an OSF/1 alpha running v1.2. I have it set up to do "lazy"
swap allocation. The sys had 82MB swap, 32MB mem. Today a user was running
two huge jobs and the thing was thrashing, so I used "swapon /dev/rz9g" to
add a second swap area interactively, of size 500MB, and modified /etc/fstab
so it will mount at boot. I then had the user:

	* let one huge job finish
	* took note of swap usage at this point with "swapon -s"
	* kill second huge job
	* took note of swap usage at intermediate point
	* restart second job
	* took note of swap usage with second job restarted

Swap usage initially was 68% of 82MB file in use, 0% of 500MB file in use.
Swap usage after killing job was 50% of 82MB, 0% of 500MB.
Swap usage after restarting job was 70% of 82MB, 0% of 500MB.

I would have expected that on restarting a large job, it would use the
new secondary page file that was empty, rather than going into the 50%
utilized original pagefile, but this was not the case.

Can someone explain OSF's rationale in assigning processes to swap space?

Also, any recommendations for a good book on OSF management, performance,
and/or internals?? I'm not looking for  a highly technical ref., but something
that will give me the basics and help me plan and manage this thing better.
Thanks very much!

