>
>Does anyone know how to get the cc compiler to treat int and unsigned as
>64 bit, to simplify porting of 32 bit applications, which assume pointers
>are the same size as int?

No way as it would break communication with almost all header files
(the size of structures would change). There is the reverse option
in the 1.3 compilers, called "-taso" (truncated address space option).

While this keeps int at 32 and pointers at 64, it makes sure that
the *value* of all pointers fits into 32 bits (hence if you assign
a 64 pointer to a int, thus truncating it and later retrieve it
again it becomes a valid address again by widening).

>Does anyone have any useful tips for porting 32 bit applications?

If you really want to port, make it 64 bit clean.  Use lint -Q to
find most of the places where thing go haywire.

>And finally, does anyone know how to switch the Alpha into 32 bit mode?

There is no 32 bit mode. The 32 bit usage of Alpha under VMS and NT is
only due to the way the compilers generate code for it. Having 32 bit
mode under OSF/1 would require all 32 bit libraries and headers to go
with it plus an adaption layer in between to get from 32 bit system
call interfaces to 64 bit interfaces. It should be noted that the latter
already exists in the MXR subsystem for supporting translated MIPS/Ultrix
applications.

