SNiFF+: takeFive Starts Free University Distribution of Commercial C/C++
Programming Environment

1. Introduction
===============
Since the beginning of 1993 takeFive has taken over development and support 
for SNiFF+, a leading edge C/C++ programming environment.  With SNiFF+ 
rapidly gaining commercial acceptance takeFive has decided to offer the 
product free to educational establishments. There are several reasons for 
this step.

* Universities are doing valuable work in eduction and research from which 
the entire computer industry profits.  takeFive/UBILAB have a strong 
academic background and would like to contribute towards this work.  

* SNiFF+ has a feature set including an easy to use WYSIWYG editor and 
powerful browser that makes the product an ideal educational tool.

* UBILAB, the developers of Sniff, the public domain predecessor of SNiFF+, 
plan  to start public domain distribution of Beyond-Sniff, at OOPSLA 93.
One of the goals of Beyond-Sniff is to offer people interested in writing 
upper and lower case tools a platform providing an object-oriented 
interface to services such as project management, configuration management, 
version control, and source code. To give Universities the possibility to 
fully exploit this platform it is important that they have an easy access
to SNiFF+, which is the basis for the Beyond Sniff project. 


2. Overview of SNiFF+
=====================
SNiFF+ is a graphical programming environment for C++.
Some of the main features of SNiFF+ are:
* Handy and easy to use due to a unique graphical user interface
* Fast and efficient even for projects with more than 500 KLOC of C++ code
* Complete management of all development tasks
* Unique browsing means always seeing the right amount of information
* Open because it integrates any compiler and debugger of choice.
* Migrators can use vanilla C and advanced C++ 3.0 even in one project
* Portable on all major UNIX platforms
  Sparc (SunOs and Solaris), HP-PA, IBM RS/6000, DEC Alpha and Ultrix
All tools of SNiFF+ are tightly integrated and have a powerful and intuitive
 user interface:
* Workspace Manager: for efficient window handling on the project level
* Project Editor: for defining even complex project structures
* Symbol Browser: for navigating through the project source code on the
  symbol level
* Class Browser: for browsing the internal structure of classes
* Source Editor: with symbol based positioning and compilation support
* Hierarchy Browser: for investigating the inheritance graph
* Cross Referencer: with unique semantic filtering
* Graphical Debugger frontend to dbx, gdb, and other debugging systems;
  directly integrating into the Editor


4. Overview of Beyond-Sniff
===========================
Persons to contact are:
Walter Bischofberger and Bruno Schaeffer, UBILAB, Union Bank of Switzerland
{Walter.Bischofberger|Bruno.Schaeffer}@ubilab.ubs.ch

4.1. Motivation
---------------
During the development of Sniff and the transfer of our know how to our 
commercial partner two requirements for the next generation of programming 
environments became more and more obvious:

* Support of cooperative software development by several teams connected by
  local and wide area networks.
* Support of a reasonable development process for object-oriented software
  systems (not todays CASE tools).

With Beyond-Sniff we want to provide cooperating teams of developers with 
support for:

* communication and coordination
* project and site management
* documentation (of classes, design patterns...)
* version control and configuration management (in a distributed
  environment)
* comparing and merging projects on a high abstraction level
* design and programming

4.2 Technical Approaches
------------------------ 
The approach with which we want to achieve our goals is based on frameworks,
services, and graphs of generic data objects.

We are currently working on a framework supporting the development of
distributed applications which coordinate their cooperation by exchanging
messages and by sharing information. Services implemented based on this
framework are, for example, the project manager service, the symbol table
service, and the annotation service.

The second framework which is developed in a cooperation with the University 
of Zurich addresses the problem of reliably managing project information 
replicated at different sites in a wide area network.

Based on the mentioned services we are currently developing tools such as a 
class browser supporting the visualization of design patterns and a syntax 
sensitive diff allowing for the comparing and merging of C and C++ source 
files and projects on the language level.

Supporting the coordination of development teams is a challenge, because it 
is less obvious how to sensibly support this activity than how to support the 
programming and documentation processes. Our approach for project 
coordination is annotation based. But our notion of annotations is more 
general than the annotations currently used in other programming 
environments. Our annotations consist of a number of links into source code 
and other project related documents, of a description consisting of text and 
graphics and of a list of key value pairs describing the annotation. All 
annotations are stored in a distributed database and can be queried with a 
comfortable database browser. With this database browser it is then possible 
to obtain project related information such as: all the error annotations 
appended to a project during the last two weeks, all the files of project X 
currently checked out by developer Y...

The annotation mechanism is also used to notify developers about certain 
events. A developer can register his interest in certain events (e.g., that 
somebody checks out a file of project X). On such an event he is notified via 
e-mail.

4.4. Time Schedule for Beyond-Sniff
-----------------------------------
The basic infrastructure is running and we will demonstrate it next week at
OOPSLA 93 in two presentations. Currently we have first implementations of
the main building blocks supporting development in TCP/IP based local area 
networks....

* the framework for distributed objects
* the annotation manager and the annotation browser
* the project service
* the symbol table service
* the Beyond-ClassBrowser (an example of how to take advantage of the
  basic services)

The first public domain release of Beyond-Sniff will be published in the
first quarter of 1994.

4.5. Distribution Strategy for Beyond-Sniff
-------------------------------------------
Because Beyond-Sniff is based on SNiFF+, we will bundle Beyond-Sniff with the
SNiFF+ distribution.
SNiFF+ is available from now on. The emerging parts of Beyond-Sniff will be
incrementally added to the packages as the project evolves. News will keep
you up to date. We will also install an e-mail list service for the
Beyond-Sniff project.

5. Prerequisites for getting SNiFF+ For FREE
============================================
Since SNiFF+ is a commercial product, which is maintained and sold to
