  I just attached our Sony eraseable-optical drive to a DEC 3000/400
workstation running OSF/1 v1.3.  The drive behaves properly (unlike
when the machine was running v1.2), but I find that the OSF/1 mount 
command is rather different than under ultrix.  I would like regular
users (non-superuser) to be able to mount their own optical drive 
platters. This was possible under ultrix as long as you set the 
protections properly on the /dev/*rz* files relevant to the optical 
drive.
  Looking at the man page for OSF/1, it states that to mount a
UFS filesystem you must be superuser.  The only way that I've
found for mount (and umount) to work for a regular user is to
set the setuid and setgid bits on the /usr/sbin/mount (or umount)
command and change ownership of the command to root.  This strikes 
me as about as unsecure as possible as any user can mount and 
umount any filesystem.  Is there a better, more secure solution to the 
problem?
  Thanks for any assistance.

