I'm running V1.3. Yes, the figures I quoted are VSZ e.g.,

grunwald   346  0.0  0.6 5.06M 616K ??  I      Aug 30   0:40.78 xcd -f /dev/rrz4c -geometry 666x138+3+346

So, in this case, I'm to assume it's really only taking ~1Mb, not 5Mb
of swap?

So far, with V1.3, I've run into the following problems:

	(1) lots of ``10% swap'' messages, before going to lazy swap
	allocation. This on a machine with ~150Mb of swap, 96Mb of ram
	and a single user. Imagine what the low-end market thinks
	about this.

	(2) Lots of ``file table overflow'' messages until I bumped
	maxusers to be 64 in the kernel. This would hang the machine.
	Again, single user most of the time.

	(3) Today, I started getting console messages about ``filehandle
	spans multiple mbufs''. I have no idea what this means, but
	it doesn't sound good.

	(4) one incident of filesystem corruption when a directory
	was deleted over NFS (from a DECstation). Not repeatable,
	thank goodness.

I understand V1.3 is a big improvement over V1.2. However, DEC is
betting the farm on Alpha, and this software is ~1 year after machine
intro.  I had expected it to be somewhat more stable by this point.

However, I'm generally happy with the machines (a built-in speaker
would have been nice). I have $ to buy more machines, and will choose
Alphas (or, more likely, the up-coming lower-end AlphaPC) over the SGI
Indy if I have anything to say about it.


