In article <1993Aug24.160134.1@summer.chem.su.oz.au>,
 <kelly_d@summer.chem.su.oz.au> wrote:
>Hi Guys,
>
>Has anyone worked out the procedure and details for adding a Sony SDT-4000
>DAT drive onto a Dec Alpha 3000/400 running OSF 1.2 ?
>
>The machine hangs during boot-up whan it tries to mount the filesystems if
>one uses the standard tz device.
>
>Thanks,
>
>Dave.

Dave,

You'll have to get the specifics from SONY about their tape drive,
and modify the device driver.  I recently did this with an Exabyte 8505
tape drive.  It seemed that the 8505 writes 5 GB compressed (10GB) format
if the default tz driver is used, as the OS does not recognize the drive
ID string.  This caused my 500 AXP to make tapes that no one else could
read.  The changes I had to make (probably so will you) was in the file
/sys/data/cam_data.c; I added a couple of device entries (for a generic
8500, an 8500C, and an 8505) and a new density table, although I could
have modified the existing one for the DEC TZK09 drive.  (This is DEC's
version of the Exabyte 8500, and had to be handled a little differently.)

Anyway, I had to get the information from Exabyte about the types of ID
that the drive returns, and the density codes it supported.  You will
need this (and probably more) from SONY.

good luck in your searches.

eyc

